WEBVTT

00:00:00.410 --> 00:00:01.660
Repeat speeding requires

00:00:01.660 --> 00:00:02.580
different decisions.

00:00:03.000 --> 00:00:04.710
Maya has a loaded delivery,

00:00:05.130 --> 00:00:05.890
worsening weather,

00:00:06.420 --> 00:00:07.250
and a tight schedule;

00:00:07.780 --> 00:00:08.940
your task is to protect

00:00:08.940 --> 00:00:09.410
control.

00:00:10.810 --> 00:00:12.540
Before departure, Maya checks

00:00:12.540 --> 00:00:12.890
weather,

00:00:13.290 --> 00:00:15.280
load, route and safe stops,

00:00:15.730 --> 00:00:17.180
then agrees realistic timing

00:00:17.180 --> 00:00:18.450
with dispatch while still

00:00:18.450 --> 00:00:18.800
parked.

00:00:20.290 --> 00:00:22.130
Rain changes the driving task.

00:00:22.550 --> 00:00:23.870
Maya lowers her speed,

00:00:24.290 --> 00:00:25.670
allows more following space,

00:00:26.270 --> 00:00:27.830
and keeps every control input

00:00:27.830 --> 00:00:29.610
smooth as conditions worsen.

00:00:31.050 --> 00:00:32.080
A wet ramp approaches.

00:00:32.630 --> 00:00:33.900
Its curve advisory cannot

00:00:33.900 --> 00:00:35.400
guarantee a safe speed for

00:00:35.400 --> 00:00:36.900
this loaded truck, and the

00:00:36.900 --> 00:00:38.120
delivery deadline changes

00:00:38.120 --> 00:00:38.490
nothing.

00:00:40.660 --> 00:00:41.990
Pause here to consider three

00:00:41.990 --> 00:00:42.480
options:

00:00:42.940 --> 00:00:43.660
maintain speed,

00:00:44.090 --> 00:00:45.130
brake within the curve,

00:00:45.620 --> 00:00:46.720
or reduce pace on the

00:00:46.720 --> 00:00:47.030
approach;

00:00:47.630 --> 00:00:49.120
which action protects control?

00:00:50.450 --> 00:00:51.810
Slow before steering into the

00:00:51.810 --> 00:00:52.130
curve.

00:00:52.620 --> 00:00:54.090
Maya reduces speed on the

00:00:54.090 --> 00:00:54.450
approach,

00:00:55.120 --> 00:00:56.730
then steers smoothly through the

00:00:56.730 --> 00:00:58.620
bend with her trailer tracking

00:00:58.620 --> 00:00:58.980
steadily.

00:01:00.380 --> 00:01:01.990
The route now drops downhill.

00:01:02.450 --> 00:01:03.170
Before descending,

00:01:03.580 --> 00:01:05.310
Maya chooses suitable speed and

00:01:05.310 --> 00:01:06.860
gear, follows the signs,

00:01:07.380 --> 00:01:08.940
and uses her approved vehicle

00:01:08.940 --> 00:01:09.380
procedure.

00:01:10.580 --> 00:01:11.950
Braking reserve can disappear.

00:01:12.450 --> 00:01:14.030
Entering too fast can demand

00:01:14.030 --> 00:01:14.860
excessive braking,

00:01:15.310 --> 00:01:16.560
building heat and reducing

00:01:16.560 --> 00:01:18.140
effectiveness just when the road

00:01:18.140 --> 00:01:19.280
requires more control.

00:01:20.500 --> 00:01:22.220
Fog is thickening ahead and

00:01:22.220 --> 00:01:23.450
forward visibility keeps

00:01:23.450 --> 00:01:23.880
shrinking;

00:01:24.360 --> 00:01:25.730
Maya judges that continued

00:01:25.730 --> 00:01:27.610
travel will soon become unsafe,

00:01:28.140 --> 00:01:29.360
even at reduced speed.

00:01:30.810 --> 00:01:32.420
Safe truck parking is available.

00:01:32.810 --> 00:01:34.610
Maya enters smoothly and parks

00:01:34.610 --> 00:01:35.920
clear of traffic before

00:01:35.920 --> 00:01:37.810
worsening fog removes this safe

00:01:37.810 --> 00:01:38.780
stopping opportunity.

00:01:40.500 --> 00:01:41.780
Dispatch needs an update.

00:01:42.280 --> 00:01:43.950
Safely parked, Maya reports

00:01:43.950 --> 00:01:44.470
conditions,

00:01:45.000 --> 00:01:46.060
confirms her location,

00:01:46.520 --> 00:01:47.530
explains the delay

00:01:47.940 --> 00:01:49.030
and agrees the next safe

00:01:49.030 --> 00:01:49.470
check-in.

00:01:50.730 --> 00:01:51.780
Make your next choice

00:01:51.780 --> 00:01:52.310
measurable.

00:01:52.780 --> 00:01:53.800
Write your pressure trigger,

00:01:54.190 --> 00:01:54.940
safe response,

00:01:55.500 --> 00:01:56.580
and the support you need,

00:01:57.020 --> 00:01:58.660
then review future trips with

00:01:58.660 --> 00:01:58.970
your coach.
